WEBVTT

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:09.420
<v Ira Glass>Sylvia's parents are immigrants, very traditional. And in Sylvia's house, the men are men, the women are women, just like back in the old country.
<v Ira Glass>西尔维亚的父母是移民，非常传统。在西尔维亚家里，男人就是男人，女人就是女人，就像在老家一样。

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:29.530
<v Sylvia>My brother goes, "Oh, I want tortillas." And my mom, just like right there on, she'll just turn off the TV and she'll go make them. And my brother goes, "I want money." And my mom's right there taking my money. He goes, "Wash this shirt for me. I want to wear it tomorrow." And there goes my mom, washing the shirt. And it's not like that with me.
<v Sylvia>我哥哥说：“哦，我想吃玉米饼。”然后我妈就会立刻关掉电视去给他做。我哥哥说：“我要钱。”我妈就在那儿拿我的钱给他。他说：“帮我洗这件衬衫，我明天要穿。”于是我妈就去洗衬衫了。但我可没这种待遇。

00:00:29.530 --> 00:01:03.660
<v Sylvia>That's the way she thinks. That's the way she is. She's like, he's a boy. For instance, he can't cook for himself. He's a boy. He can't do this because he's a boy. That's a woman's job. My mom always has this little saying that really annoys me. Sometimes when the house is dirty, she says, "Oh, it looks like there's never been women in the house," making it sound like women are supposed to clean. And I'm thinking, Dad can clean. She goes, "No, he's supposed to be in the garage fixing the car or something."
<v Sylvia>她就是这么想的，她就是那样的人。她觉得，他是男孩，所以他不会做饭，他不能做这个，因为他是男孩。那是女人的活儿。我妈总挂在嘴边的一句话让我特别反感。有时候家里乱了，她会说：“噢，看来这家里从来没有过女人啊。”这话听起来就好像女人天生就该干家务似的。我心想，我爸也可以打扫啊。她说：“不行，他应该在车库修车什么的。”

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:37.800
<v Ira Glass>It's a typical American story in this country. From the time she was little, Sylvia spoke English better than her parents. She was the one in the family who'd call the phone company or the utilities. She translated teacher conferences. If the family was going somewhere and needed directions, Sylvia was the one who would walk up to a stranger and ask for them. And now, nearly grown up, she wants to be an American girl in a way that her parents don't completely understand. She goes to a big, integrated public school. A few years ago, she started listening to The Cranberries and Nirvana and Metallica, not the kind of stuff her parents knew growing up in small towns in rural Mexico.
<v Ira Glass>在这个国家，这是一个典型的美国故事。西尔维亚从小英语就比她父母好。家里要打给电话公司或水电局，都是她来联络。家长会由她来翻译。如果一家人出门需要问路，也是西尔维亚去向陌生人问路。如今，快要长大的她想成为一名美国女孩，但她的父母并不完全理解。她就读于一所大型的综合性公立学校。几年前，她开始听小红莓乐队、涅槃乐队和金属乐队的歌，这些可不是她父母在墨西哥农村小镇长大时听的那种音乐。

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:49.160
<v Sylvia>My mom wants me to be a typical Mexican girl. When I was younger, before I had my cotillion, I used to start liking alternative music.
<v Sylvia>我妈希望我成为一个典型的墨西哥女孩。我小时候，在举行成人礼之前，我开始喜欢上另类音乐。

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:52.180
<v Ira Glass>Cotillion's like a coming out party when you turn 15.
<v Ira Glass>成人礼就像你15岁时的社交首秀。

00:01:52.180 --> 00:02:24.390
<v Sylvia>Yeah, when you turn 15. You have a huge party. You get your own beautiful dress. It's long, and it's big. I started liking alternative music around the age of 14, around the time they started making my cotillion. And I remember my cousins used to say, oh, as soon as you hit your cotillion, you're going to start liking Mexican music. And we're going to start taking you out. Because in my family, as soon as you hit 15, you're allowed to go to Mexican dances. But you usually go with your older cousins.
<v Sylvia>对，15岁的时候。你会举办一场盛大的派对。你会穿上属于你自己的漂亮裙子，裙子又长又大。我在14岁左右开始喜欢另类音乐，当时他们正准备为我举办成人礼。我记得我的表亲们总说，等你过了成人礼，你就会开始喜欢墨西哥音乐了，我们也会开始带你出去玩。因为在我们家，一到15岁，你就可以去参加墨西哥舞会了。但通常你是和年长的表亲一起去。

00:02:24.390 --> 00:03:01.060
<v Sylvia>And that's where my mom wanted in me. My mom wanted me to be like my cousins. They went to Mexican dances. They had Mexican boyfriends. I mean, she wanted me to dress like them. She didn't want me to dress kind of alternative. And now sometimes we get into fights and I tell my mom, I'm not like my cousins. I'm like, my cousins are already like 19, 18, and they're already pregnant or married. I'm like, is that what you want me to do with my life?
<v Sylvia>我妈希望我成为那样的人。她希望我像我的表亲一样。她们会去参加墨西哥舞会，交墨西哥男朋友。我的意思是，她希望我的穿着打扮也像她们。她不希望我穿得那么另类。现在我们有时会吵架，我告诉她，我跟我的表亲不一样。我说，我的表亲们都已经18、19岁，不是怀孕就是结婚了。我说，这就是你希望我过的人生吗？

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:23.960
<v Ira Glass>This weekend, this is a particularly urgent question in Sylvia's life. This weekend, January 31, 1998, Sylvia turns 18. She's legally recognized as an adult, capable of deciding for herself what she'll do with her life. And she and her mom have been talking about what she's going to do. Sylvia wants to go to a four year college, wait to get married, wait to have kids. And her mom is trying to understand.
<v Ira Glass>这个周末，这对西尔维亚来说是一个特别紧迫的问题。这个周末，即1998年1月31日，西尔维亚就满18岁了。她在法律上被视为成年人，有能力决定自己的人生道路。她和她妈妈一直在讨论她未来要做什么。西尔维亚想上四年制大学，晚点结婚，晚点要孩子。而她妈妈正在努力去理解。

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:47.980
<v Sylvia>Sometimes she's kind of like, yeah, do what you want. Do what you want. Become whatever you want. And there's just times where, like, why do you want to do that? Why do you want to do that? Why do you think you're better than everybody? Why do you think you're special? I'm like, Ma, I don't think I'm special. I just want to do something with my life.
<v Sylvia>有时她会说，随你吧，做你想做的，成为你想成为的人。但有时她又会说，你为什么要那样做？你为什么要那样做？你为什么觉得自己比别人强？你为什么觉得自己很特别？我说，妈，我不觉得我特别，我只是想在人生中做点事情。

00:03:47.980 --> 00:04:06.500
<v Ira Glass>When her mom was young, back in Tamaulipas, her mom wanted to go to school. She was admitted to a good school nearby, but her grandfather told her no. He said the only reason the girls go to school is to get boyfriends. So she stopped going in sixth grade. Now she spends most of her time at home, raising Sylvia's brother and sister, taking care of the house, rarely leaving the house.
<v Ira Glass>她妈年轻时在塔毛利帕斯州也想上学。她被附近一所好学校录取了，但她祖父不让去。他说女孩子上学唯一的目的就是交男朋友。于是她六年级就辍学了。现在她大部分时间都待在家里，照顾西尔维亚的弟弟妹妹，操持家务，很少出门。

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:28.530
<v Sylvia>My mom has lived in a box all her life, and I feel like a lot of Mexican women have. When you live in a box, you raise your children in a box. And sometimes I'll just try to climb out. And she's like pushing me in or I'm trying to poke a hole in the box and she tapes it right back up.
<v Sylvia>我妈一生都活在一个盒子里，我觉得很多墨西哥女性也是。当你活在一个盒子里，你养育孩子时也会让他们待在盒子里。有时我试着爬出来，她就把我推回去，或者我想在盒子上戳个洞，她马上又会把它封上。

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:46.050
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our radio program, Escaping the Box You were Born Into. From WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International, it's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week, of course, we choose a theme, bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's program in three acts. Act One, An American Girl Turns 18.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的广播节目中，主题是《逃离你出生的盒子》。这里是来自芝加哥WBEZ和国际公共广播电台的《这就是美国生活》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周我们都会选择一个主题，带来各种相关的不同故事。今天的节目分为三幕。第一幕，一位美国女孩年满18岁。

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:58.170
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, $33 Million Box, the true story of a successful Cincinnati businessman millionaire who escaped his old life and created a new one in one single day by robbing a bank.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，3300万美元的盒子，讲述了一位成功的辛辛那提百万富翁商人，在一天之内通过抢劫银行，彻底逃离旧生活并开启新生活的真实故事。

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:14.170
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, The Feeling You Want and the Feeling You Get. We have an excerpt from David Cale's funny and thrilling story of a middle-aged woman who escapes the box of her own life. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，你想要的感受与你得到的感受。我们节选了大卫·凯尔的一段有趣又惊心动魄的故事，讲述了一位中年女性如何逃离她自己生活的盒子。请继续收听。

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:28.560
<v Ira Glass>Act One, American Girl Turns 18. So Sylvia is living at home, senior in high school, engaged in an ongoing discussion with her mom about what she should be after graduation in four months and whether she'll live a life like her mom's.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，美国女孩年满18岁。西尔维亚住在家里，是高三学生。她正和妈妈讨论毕业后该做什么，以及她是否会过上像她妈妈那样的生活。

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:56.180
<v Sylvia>She's too scared to get out of the box. She is. She even told me. She's just too scared. I mean, if she has to go to my godmother's house, all she has to do do is take one bus and go straight. And she just gets off at a certain stop and walks a block or two. That scares my mom that she's by herself. It scares the hell out of her being by herself. It just really scares her.
<v Sylvia>她太害怕走出那个盒子了。真的，她亲口对我说的。她就是太害怕了。我的意思是，如果她要去我教母家，她只需坐一趟直达公交车，然后在某一站下车走一两个街区就行。就这样，她妈妈都害怕独自出门。独自一人让她感到恐惧，真的让她非常害怕。

00:05:56.180 --> 00:06:27.470
<v Sylvia>And I'm always by myself. And I'm always doing things by myself. And I'm always doing things that I want. And I think sometimes she admires me that I'm not scared, but at the same time, she just doesn't-- it's like she admires me that I'm not scared, but I think at the same time she hates me because she's scared.
<v Sylvia>而我总是独来独往。我总是独自做事情，做我想要做的事情。我觉得有时候她很佩服我没有恐惧感，但同时，我觉得她又恨我，因为她自己充满恐惧。

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:32.260
<v Ira Glass>Describe the box that she's in.
<v Ira Glass>描述一下她所处的那个盒子。

00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:53.870
<v Sylvia>I would guess it's just the typical Mexican family, where you're married and you have children and you die together. And you travel once in a while to your homeland, and you have usually Mexican music and laughter and drinking and partying. And all the cousins coming together and all the aunts coming together.
<v Sylvia>我觉得那就是典型的墨西哥家庭，结婚、生子，然后一起老去。偶尔回故乡旅行，总是听着墨西哥音乐，欢笑、喝酒、聚会。所有的堂表亲聚在一起，所有的姑姨聚在一起。

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:57.860
<v Ira Glass>Do you think she's unhappy? I mean, that sounds like it could be actually kind of nice.
<v Ira Glass>你觉得她不快乐吗？听起来其实挺美好的。

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:16.140
<v Sylvia>Yeah, it could be nice, but, I mean, when you only hang around with that kind of people and that certain people and they're-- because they're kind of also-- not like bigots or something, but they're also really-- kind of like the way she thinks.
<v Sylvia>是啊，确实可能挺美好。但问题是，当你只和那种人、那种特定群体交往时——他们并不是偏执狂之类的，但他们确实……就像我妈那样思考。

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:17.260
<v Ira Glass>Everybody thinks the same way.
<v Ira Glass>每个人都以同样的方式思考。

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:18.740
<v Sylvia>Everybody thinks the same way.
<v Sylvia>每个人都以同样的方式思考。

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:21.060
<v Ira Glass>And they're not so crazy about people who think differently.
<v Ira Glass>而且他们对于想法不同的人不太包容。

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:54.150
<v Sylvia>Yeah, it's kind of like I'm the outcast of the family. I'm like the black sheep. That's why I really never depended on my parents because I really never had them when I wanted them. And also I never really asked for anything. I never really wanted anything from them. And now that I'm almost going to turn 18, they noticed that I really don't ask anything from them.
<v Sylvia>是啊，我就像是家里的局外人。我是那只“黑羊”。这就是为什么我从不依赖我的父母，因为当我需要他们的时候，他们从来不在。而且我从不向他们要求什么，我也不想要他们的东西。现在我快满18岁了，他们才发现我真的什么都没向他们索取过。

00:07:54.150 --> 00:08:55.260
<v Sylvia>I remember one time. It's really specific. It happened last year. It just popped into my head. I remember that my grandparents come every summer. And all the uncles and aunts come to the house because our grandparents are there and stuff like that. And I was working, and when I came home, one of my aunts came. She's the second oldest in the family, and I find her really bitter. She's my aunt, and I love her and stuff like that, but she's really bitter. And she had told my mom that really got me mad. This woman who I talk to maybe once or twice a year. And because my mom was raised by this aunt-- she sees her as a mother figure, too-- she has a lot of respect for her. She told my mom that the day that I grew up, I'm going to be ashamed of my parents and especially of her because she has no education and because she's an immigrant of this country.
<v Sylvia>我还记得一件事，非常具体。那是去年发生的，突然浮现在我脑海里。我记得祖父母每年夏天都会来。所有的叔叔阿姨都会来家里，因为祖父母在。我当时在工作，回来时遇到了一位阿姨。她是家里第二大的长辈，我觉得她非常刻薄。她是我的阿姨，我也爱她之类的，但她真的很刻薄。她对我妈说了一些话，让我非常生气。那个女人我一年可能才说上一两次话。因为我妈是由这个阿姨抚养大的——她也把她看作母亲般的长辈——所以她非常尊重她。她对我妈说，等我长大了，我会以我的父母为耻，尤其是以她为耻，因为她没有受过教育，是个移民。

00:08:55.260 --> 00:09:35.060
<v Sylvia>And I told my mom, how dare she say that? She knows nothing about me. Does she want me to become like her kids? Her oldest daughter got pregnant at 16. And her youngest son is kind of like a drifter. He really doesn't know what he does. And I'm like, this woman has the idea that I'm going to be ashamed of my mom because I'm going to have an education and because I'm going to have a career?
<v Sylvia>我对我妈说，她怎么敢这么说？她根本不了解我。她想让我变成她那样的孩子吗？她的大女儿16岁就怀孕了。她的小儿子是个流浪汉，根本不知道自己在干什么。我心想，这个女人竟然认为我会因为自己受过教育、有事业而以我的母亲为耻？

00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:51.180
<v Ira Glass>I suggest to Sylvia that her parents might understand her situation better than she acknowledges. After all, they themselves escaped the box of their own early lives, uprooted themselves from rural Mexico to inner city Chicago, to a country where they didn't even speak the language. Sylvia doesn't buy it.
<v Ira Glass>我向西尔维亚指出，她的父母可能比她意识到的更了解她的处境。毕竟，他们自己也逃离了早年生活的盒子，从墨西哥农村移居到芝加哥市中心，来到一个连语言都不通的国家。西尔维亚不买账。

00:09:51.180 --> 00:10:04.830
<v Ira Glass>But as we talk, one of the most striking things is how there's still a part of her, the biggest part of her, that wants her parents to simply understand her and how she sees her own life. She still wants to be part of the family.
<v Ira Glass>但在我们交谈时，最令人印象深刻的一点是，她内心深处仍有一部分——也是最大的一部分——希望父母能真正理解她以及她对生活的看法。她依然想成为家庭的一员。

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:20.860
<v Sylvia>It's like this, sometimes you usually see my mom and my brother talking and stuff like that, and they're goofing off. You see my little sister-- sorry.
<v Sylvia>就像这样，有时你会看到我妈和我哥哥在聊天什么的，他们就在那儿胡闹。你会看到我的小妹妹——对不起。

00:10:20.860 --> 00:10:22.130
<v Ira Glass>Do you want some water?
<v Ira Glass>你想喝点水吗？

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:44.540
<v Sylvia>No, that's OK. Usually I see my little sister and my dad talking and playing. Or sometimes you see my mom and my little sister laughing and stuff like that. And I'm usually in another room doing my own thing. I usually never do anything with her.
<v Sylvia>不用，没关系。通常我会看到我的小妹妹和我爸爸在聊天玩耍。或者有时你会看到我妈和我小妹妹在那儿笑之类的。而我通常在另一个房间做我自己的事。我通常从不和她一起参与什么活动。

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:50.230
<v Ira Glass>And do you feel like that's mostly your choice, that that's the way that you want it?
<v Ira Glass>你觉得这主要是你自己的选择吗？是你想要的那种方式吗？

00:10:50.230 --> 00:11:13.570
<v Sylvia>Sometimes I do because a lot of the things that they want me to do, I don't want to do. And it really hurts me that they're not really supportive. So I guess I just try to move myself away from them so they won't hurt me as much. But they don't understand that. They think I'm too strong to be hurt.
<v Sylvia>有时是的，因为很多他们想让我做的事情，我并不想做。而且他们不支持我，这让我很受伤。所以我猜我只是试着让自己远离他们，这样他们就不会伤害我那么深了。但他们不明白这一点。他们以为我坚强到不会受伤。

00:11:13.570 --> 00:11:21.270
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. Yeah, but I mean they're not right about that.
<v Ira Glass>是的。是的，但他们在这点上错了。

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:25.070
<v Sylvia>No, they're not.
<v Sylvia>不，他们错了。

00:11:25.070 --> 00:11:36.850
<v Ira Glass>But getting out of the box doesn't necessarily mean you're any happier. There are plenty of people who start off in a life that's really in a box and get out of it and change all the time. And that doesn't necessarily mean you're going to be happy.
<v Ira Glass>但逃离盒子并不一定意味着你更快乐。有很多人起初过着像在盒子里一样的生活，后来跳了出来，生活也发生了改变。但这并不意味着你一定会幸福。

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:38.370
<v Sylvia>Yeah.
<v Sylvia>是的。

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:42.600
<v Ira Glass>You can be happy in the box or out of the box.
<v Ira Glass>无论在盒子里还是盒子外，你都可以快乐。

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:57.850
<v Sylvia>It all depends on how you feel.
<v Sylvia>这完全取决于你自己的感受。

00:11:57.850 --> 00:12:02.400
<v Ira Glass>Do you have a sense of what kind of job you might want to yet?
<v Ira Glass>你对自己将来想做什么样的工作有头绪了吗？

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:10.320
<v Sylvia>I have an idea. I want this job that-- because I want to do digital effects, special digital effects.
<v Sylvia>我有想法。我想要那种……因为我想做数字特效，特殊的数字特效。

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:12.020
<v Ira Glass>Like for movies and stuff.
<v Ira Glass>就像电影特效之类的？

00:12:12.020 --> 00:12:56.750
<v Sylvia>Yeah, like Jurassic Park and Men in Black and stuff like that. I think it's so cool. I just want one day to have a job. I usually think of myself sitting, having not like an office to myself but usually have an open space with a lot of different equipment everywhere and sitting in front of the computer and doing all these things on the computer and animating. And getting really frustrated because it takes me like six months to do like a five-second little scene. It's just doing my own thing, not have to get dressed up, go casual, have an eyebrow ring or something on, how my hair whatever color I want, have a real cool boss. But that's just a dream.
<v Sylvia>对，就像《侏罗纪公园》、《黑衣人》那样的。我觉得酷毙了。我梦想有一天能有份工作。我脑海里的场景是：不是一个人在封闭办公室里，而是一个有很多不同设备的大开间，我坐在电脑前，做着各种电脑动画。因为做一个五秒钟的小片段可能需要六个月而感到非常沮丧。我只想做我自己的事，不用刻意打扮，穿休闲装，戴个眉钉，头发染成任何我喜欢的颜色，有个超酷的老板。但这只是个梦想。

00:12:56.750 --> 00:13:03.860
<v Ira Glass>Have you seen those specials on TV where they show here's how they make these special effects and you see the people and what they look like who do all that work?
<v Ira Glass>你看过电视上的那些特辑吗？就是展示这些特效是怎么制作的，你也能看到从事这些工作的人长什么样。

00:13:03.860 --> 00:13:25.070
<v Sylvia>I've seen the making of Jurassic Park and Titanic and the Space Jam . And that's what I want to do, just use my imagination and make this thing blow up or something.
<v Sylvia>我看过《侏罗纪公园》、《泰坦尼克号》和《太空大灌篮》的幕后制作。那就是我想做的，只靠想象力去创造，让某个东西爆炸什么的。

00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:26.690
<v Ira Glass>What's your biggest fear in trying to get out of the box?
<v Ira Glass>你在尝试逃离盒子时，最大的恐惧是什么？

00:13:26.690 --> 00:13:47.680
<v Sylvia>Failing. That's my biggest fear is failing. Because I've done so many things. I've done so many organizations. I've done so many clubs. I did all these classes and all these grades. And it's just going to go to waste. That's my biggest fear that it's going to go to waste, that all that I did is going to amount to nothing.
<v Sylvia>失败。我最大的恐惧就是失败。因为我做了太多事情。我参加过那么多组织，进过那么多社团。我完成了所有的课程，拿到了所有的分数。这一切如果付诸东流……我最大的恐惧就是这些努力全都白费，我做的一切最终变得一文不值。

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:51.170
<v Ira Glass>And if you failed, what would it be? Like what would you be?
<v Ira Glass>如果你失败了，会怎样？比如你会变成什么样？

00:13:51.170 --> 00:13:52.570
<v Sylvia>What would be my failure?
<v Sylvia>什么才算我的失败？

00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:55.160
<v Ira Glass>What would happen to you? Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>你会发生什么？对。

00:13:55.160 --> 00:14:07.210
<v Sylvia>I would become working an $11 an hour job at like 25 with already two kids and a husband and just doing that, day by day doing the same thing over and over.
<v Sylvia>我可能会在25岁时做着时薪11美元的工作，已经有了两个孩子和一个丈夫，日复一日地重复着同样的生活。

00:14:07.210 --> 00:14:08.430
<v Ira Glass>And what could make you fail?
<v Ira Glass>什么会导致你失败？

00:14:08.430 --> 00:14:45.080
<v Sylvia>I don't know. Sometimes I feel like my ambition's going to die out. It's kind of like you rebel against everybody because you think you know what you're doing and you know you want to achieve more and you want to do more. But every time you take another step, it just hits you right in  the face like, ha, ha, ha, you can't do it. Go back into your line. And after a while, you just get really tired of all the closing of the doors and telling you, no, you can't do this. And you're like, well, maybe they are right. One day I'll just get tired and I'll just say, you win.
<v Sylvia>不知道。有时我觉得我的野心会磨灭。这就像是你反抗所有人，因为你觉得你知道自己在干什么，你想取得更多成就，想做更多事。但每当你向前迈进一步，现实就会迎面给你一击，仿佛在说：哈哈，你做不到的，回到你的队伍里去吧。过了一段时间，你就会对那些不断关上的门感到厌倦，所有人都告诉你“不，你不能做这个”。然后你就会想，也许他们是对的。总有一天我会厌倦，然后说：你们赢了。

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:54.010
<v Ira Glass>And do you feel like if you don't get out by the time you're 22 or 23 or 24, it's probably at that's when it would all die, like that's when your ambition would die and you would stay?
<v Ira Glass>你觉得如果你在22岁、23岁或24岁之前没有走出去，那个时候野心就会磨灭，然后你就留下了吗？

00:14:54.010 --> 00:15:33.870
<v Sylvia>Yeah, unless I change or something, unless all of a sudden something happens and my parents are supportive. And I don't get into fights as much anymore and I feel like I'm actually wanted around the house or something, and then I feel like I'm happy, like I'm actually happy to be home. Then I wouldn't mind, but I just hope that when I actually do achieve what I want, that is, I want them to say, yeah, she did it. She did it. I guess I was wrong.
<v Sylvia>是的，除非我改变了，或者突然发生了什么事，让我父母变得支持我。如果我不再跟他们吵架，觉得家里欢迎我，觉得回家的感觉真好，那我可能也不会介意。但我只希望当我真的实现了梦想时，他们能说：是的，她做到了，她真的做到了。我想我之前错了。

00:15:33.870 --> 00:17:08.010
<v Ira Glass>Sylvia turns 18 this weekend. Happy birthday. Good luck.
<v Ira Glass>西尔维亚这个周末就满18岁了。生日快乐，祝你好运。

00:17:08.010 --> 00:18:01.170
<v Ira Glass>Act Two. You know who it's been a hard week for? It's been a hard week to be an intern. In fact, you could reasonably argue that in the history of interns, this past week or two, in late January 1998, they have been the low point. An intern here at WBEZ Chicago, home of This American Life , told me that she was waiting for the L train this week. And she's standing there, and this guy strikes up a conversation. And they're talking about this and that. And he says to her at some point, so what do you do? And she tells him that she's an intern. And he says, oh, really? As if she'd just told him, my job, I'm a slut. That's my job.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕。你知道谁这周过得很艰难吗？做实习生这一周太难了。事实上，你完全可以说，在实习生历史上，1998年1月底的这两周简直是低谷。在WBEZ芝加哥（《这就是美国生活》的诞生地），一位实习生告诉我，她这周在等地铁，站着的时候，有个男人过来搭讪。他们聊着聊着，男人问她：“你是做什么的？”她告诉他自己是实习生。男人说：“哦，真的吗？”那语气简直就像她说的是：“我的工作是当荡妇。”

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:37.410
<v Ira Glass>Our senior editor Paul Tough was once an intern. I started in public radio as an intern at National Public Radio in Washington 19 years ago. In fact, I would not have escaped the box that I grew up in if not for that internship. I would not be in radio at all. We at This American Life are a staff of former interns. Producers Alix Spiegel, Nancy Updike, Julie Snyder, all former interns. And so, to strike a small blow for the dignity of interns everywhere in this dire, dire hour our own This American Life intern, the capable and serious-minded Rachel Day will introduce our next act here on the program.
<v Ira Glass>我们的资深编辑保罗·塔夫曾是一名实习生。我19年前在华盛顿的国家公共广播电台开始做实习生。事实上，如果不是那次实习，我根本无法逃离我长大的那个盒子。我根本不会进入广播行业。我们《这就是美国生活》全员都是前实习生。制片人阿利克斯·斯皮格尔、南希·厄普代克、朱莉·斯奈德，都是前实习生。所以，为了在这一严峻的时刻为全世界实习生的尊严做出一点微薄的贡献，我们《这就是美国生活》那位能力出众、认真负责的实习生瑞秋·戴，将为大家介绍本节目的下一幕。

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:38.850
<v Ira Glass>Welcome to the show, Rachel.
<v Ira Glass>欢迎来到节目组，瑞秋。

00:18:38.850 --> 00:18:40.540
<v Rachel Day>Thank you, Ira.
<v Rachel Day>谢谢，艾拉。

00:18:40.540 --> 00:18:41.940
<v Ira Glass>OK. You ready to do this?
<v Ira Glass>好的。准备好了吗？

00:18:41.940 --> 00:18:42.730
<v Rachel Day>I think so.
<v Rachel Day>我想是的。

00:18:42.730 --> 00:18:49.210
<v Ira Glass>All right. Here we go. Here's the keys to the radio show. Drive safe. Bring it back home in one piece.
<v Ira Glass>好吧。开始吧。这是节目的控制权。开车小心点，把它完整地带回来。

00:18:49.210 --> 00:18:51.590
<v Rachel Day>I think we're ready to go.
<v Rachel Day>我想我们准备好了。

00:18:51.590 --> 00:19:22.810
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, $33 Million Box. Barry Miller didn't have the kind of life you'd think he'd want to escape. He owned a clothing store in Cincinnati that was worth $33 million. He could buy whatever he wanted, took $15,000 vacations with his family. But he was unhappy. And one day, he permanently escaped his old life and entered his new one with one simple and unorthodox act. He robbed a bank. Alex Blumberg talked with him at the Hocking Correctional Facility in Nelsonville, Ohio.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，3300万美元的盒子。巴里·米勒的生活其实并不像那种你想逃离的生活。他在辛辛那提拥有一家价值3300万美元的服装店。他可以买任何他想要的东西，带家人去进行1.5万美元的度假。但他不快乐。有一天，他通过一个简单且离经叛道的行为，彻底逃离了旧生活，进入了新生活。他抢劫了一家银行。亚历克斯·布隆伯格在俄亥俄州尼尔森维尔的霍金管教所采访了他。

00:19:22.810 --> 00:19:40.340
<v Alex Blumberg>If you ask Barry Miller what was wrong with making lots of money, he'll tell you this. He used to go to Rome at least twice a year, but he never saw the Coliseum. He never went to the Vatican. If his business was over in the morning, he'd find a flight back in the afternoon. He was driven. His relationship with his family suffered.
<v Alex Blumberg>如果你问巴里·米勒赚大钱有什么问题，他会告诉你这个：他过去每年至少去两次罗马，但他从来没看过斗兽场。他从来没去过梵蒂冈。如果早晨生意处理完了，他下午就会找航班回去。他就是这么拼。他与家人的关系很糟糕。

00:19:40.340 --> 00:19:59.790
<v Barry Miller>In Dallas once, I was with my oldest son, my middle child. And I never spent a lot of time with him. I would try to go to his soccer games or those kinds of things, but I never really made it on time. I got there at the last quarter.
<v Barry Miller>有一次在达拉斯，我和我的大儿子、二儿子在一起。我从没花太多时间陪他们。我会试着去参加他们的足球比赛之类的，但我从没准时赶到过。我到的时候通常已经是最后一节了。

00:19:59.790 --> 00:20:34.880
<v Barry Miller>So we were out. We were driving around. And here I am with my son, and I have so very little to talk to him about and so little that I can relate with him about. We started talking about cars. And so I thought, well, he's interested in cars. Let's go buy a car. So I went to buy a car. We bought a car. Didn't need a car. I could afford to buy a car. We went to buy a car.
<v Barry Miller>所以我们当时开车到处逛。我和我儿子在一起，却发现几乎无话可谈，没什么共同点。我们开始聊车。我想，好吧，他对车感兴趣。那我们去买辆车吧。于是我去买车。我们买了一辆车。根本不需要车。我买得起车。我们就去买了。

00:20:34.880 --> 00:20:54.400
<v Alex Blumberg>Looking back, he says he was shallow and vain. He says he was worshiping false gods. At the time though, he only felt a vague nagging that something wasn't right. But he says gradually that feeling became more insistent and took stranger forms. For instance, he says, he started blacking out, losing hours in the day.
<v Alex Blumberg>回过头来看，他说他当时肤浅又虚荣。他说他在崇拜伪神。但当时，他只是感到一种隐约的、挥之不去的预感，觉得什么地方不对劲。他说这种感觉逐渐变得更加强烈，并以奇怪的形式表现出来。例如，他说他开始断片，一天里会丢失好几个小时的时间。

00:20:54.400 --> 00:21:18.260
<v Barry Miller>I was in New York on business, and I lost an afternoon. I didn't really know where I was, what I was doing. I ended up finding myself sitting in a lounge in a building that I recall going into because I had some business to do there, but it was three hours later. I don't know what I did for those three hours.
<v Barry Miller>我在纽约出差，丢失了一个下午的时间。我真的不知道我在哪里，在做什么。最后我发现自己坐在一个休息室里，那是某个我记得自己进去办过事的大楼，但那是三个小时之后了。我不知道那三个小时里我做了什么。

00:21:18.260 --> 00:21:41.030
<v Alex Blumberg>Sometimes you actually make a real decision to change your life and you set about doing it. But just as often, you find yourself sort of edging toward the new life, half consciously. Part of you goes on as you always have while another part secretly plots an ambush. Barry Miller's case is as extreme as you can get. When he describes what happened, it's like he's describing a person in a trance.
<v Alex Blumberg>有时你真的会做一个改变生活的决定，并着手去做。但同样常见的是，你会半自觉地朝着新生活走去。你的一部分像往常一样生活，而另一部分却在暗中策划伏击。巴里·米勒的情况是极端中的极端。当他描述发生的事情时，就像是在描述一个处于恍惚状态的人。

00:21:41.030 --> 00:22:02.740
<v Barry Miller>The day before the bank robbery, I drove by a car rental place. And I stopped. And I rented a car. Now, I have two cars, one I used and one my wife used. I rented a car. I really don't know why I rented the car, but I rented the car.
<v Barry Miller>抢劫银行的前一天，我开车经过一家汽车租赁处。我停下来，租了一辆车。我有两辆车，一辆我用，一辆我妻子用。我租了一辆车。我真的不知道为什么要租车，但我就是租了。

00:22:02.740 --> 00:22:52.900
<v Barry Miller>And I continued on my way home, and I recall that we were out of fax paper. And I drive by a Sam's, which is where we used to get our fax paper from. And Sam's is on the right side of the road, and on the left side of the road is a Kmart. So I'm headed toward Sam's to get the fax paper, and I turn into the Kmart lot instead of the Sam's lot. And I bought a ski mask. And this is September, so they're not really out for sale. And I had ski masks at home. I bought a ski mask, and I know I bought the ski mask because at some point in time I come to and I'm sitting in the car and here I have a package with a ski mask in it.
<v Barry Miller>我继续往家开，记起传真纸用完了。我开车经过一家萨姆会员店，以前我们就是在那买传真纸。萨姆会员店在路右侧，左侧是凯马特。我本来要去萨姆会员店买纸，结果却转进了凯马特的停车场。我在那买了一个滑雪面罩。当时是九月份，还没到卖这个的时候。家里明明有滑雪面罩。我买了一个，我知道我买了，因为我在某个时刻清醒过来，坐在车里，手里拿着一个装着滑雪面罩的包裹。

00:22:52.900 --> 00:22:54.380
<v Alex Blumberg>So you don't remember buying it?
<v Alex Blumberg>所以你不记得买它了？

00:22:54.380 --> 00:23:08.330
<v Barry Miller>I don't remember buying it, but I know I bought it.
<v Barry Miller>我不记得买的过程，但我知道我买了。

00:23:08.330 --> 00:23:57.800
<v Barry Miller>The next day, I get up. And I put into my briefcase a gun and a box of bullets. And regress a bit. I bought this gun on June 16 of '95. That would have been 15 months before I used it. I bought the gun because I was going to commit suicide. I had taken the gun. We had a balcony on this condo. And I took the gun one night, and I shot it into the woods. I had never shot a gun in my life. I wanted to know how I felt because I figured if I'm going to put it to my head, I wanted to know where to put it.
<v Barry Miller>第二天，我起床。在公文包里放了一把枪和一盒子弹。往回说一下，这把枪是我在1995年6月16日买的。那是我使用它的15个月前。买枪是因为我想自杀。我拿了枪。当时公寓有个阳台。一天晚上我拿枪对着森林开了一枪。我这辈子没开过枪。我想知道感觉如何，因为如果我要对着自己的头开枪，我想知道该对着哪儿。

00:23:57.800 --> 00:24:21.600
<v Barry Miller>15 months later, I get the gun, put it in my briefcase. I leave. And from there I don't really remember exactly what happened. I don't know how I got to the bank. I don't know how I picked that bank or that location. I don't recall specific incidences.
<v Barry Miller>15个月后，我带上枪，放进公文包。我出发了。从那以后我不记得具体发生了什么。不知道怎么到的银行。不知道怎么选的那家银行或那个地点。我不记得具体的细节。

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:29.590
<v Alex Blumberg>Do you remember what you said to the teller? Do you remember?
<v Alex Blumberg>你记得对柜员说了什么吗？你记得吗？

00:24:29.590 --> 00:24:50.250
<v Barry Miller>Not exactly. She said at the trial that I said something, give me all of your money, typical what you see in movies. I don't really remember. She said, give me all your money. She hadn't given me all of the money, and I said, all of it or something like that. I really don't remember. What I remember is only from what she said at the trial.
<v Barry Miller>不完全记得。她在审判时说我说了些话，比如“把钱都给我”，就像电影里看到的那样。我真的记不清了。她说我说“把钱都给我”。她没把钱全部给我，我就说“全部”。我真的不记得了。我记得的只是她在审判时说的那些。

00:24:50.250 --> 00:25:20.120
<v Alex Blumberg>When he robbed the bank, he was earning well into six figures a year. His take from the robbery, $8,000. It's a lot to swallow, really, rob a bank, then claim not to remember it, especially when you bought a ski mask and rented a car the day before. And it's possible that Miller made up the story about not remembering when he went to trial.
<v Alex Blumberg>抢劫银行时，他每年的收入高达六位数。他从抢劫中得手了8000美元。这真的很难让人接受，抢劫银行，然后声称不记得了，尤其是在前一天还买了滑雪面罩和租了车的情况下。米勒在受审时可能编造了关于失忆的故事。

00:25:20.120 --> 00:25:44.440
<v Alex Blumberg>He plead innocent by reason of insanity. The judge didn't buy it. I talked to Steve Tolbert, the prosecutor in Miller's criminal trial. He says it's possible that Miller was in a kind of a haze when he robbed the bank. He'd certainly never heard that defense before. But for Tolbert, it was immaterial. Either way, Miller was guilty of the crime.
<v Alex Blumberg>他以精神错乱为由进行无罪辩护。法官没信。我采访了米勒刑事审判的检察官史蒂夫·托尔伯特。他说米勒抢劫时可能确实处于一种恍惚状态。他以前确实没听过这种辩护。但对托尔伯特来说，这无关紧要。无论如何，米勒都犯了罪。

00:25:44.440 --> 00:26:02.320
<v Alex Blumberg>But even Tolbert questions why a man who could have gotten a substantial loan from any bank in the city would actually need to hold one up for money. And more importantly, why would he have bungled the job so badly? When Miller lays out the facts, it's hard to believe this workaholic, super achiever was really trying to get away with it.
<v Alex Blumberg>但连托尔伯特都质疑，一个可以在城里任何银行获得大额贷款的人，为什么要真的去抢银行？更重要的是，他为什么把事情搞得这么糟？当米勒列出事实时，很难相信这个工作狂、超级成就者真的想逃脱法律制裁。

00:26:02.320 --> 00:26:48.970
<v Barry Miller>Again, if this was a robbery to really get some money, I got to think I would have selected a place that wasn't on a busy street, a busy corner with a police station two blocks away. But in any event, my most vivid memories are after the bank robbery, where here I am, walking down the street that's relatively busy. The parking lot to the bank where I parked my car is in the back. I mean, I didn't even park in front. I had to walk a quarter of a block to get to the car. And I'm walking with a gun in my hand and a mask on my face at whatever time that was in the morning. It wasn't Halloween.
<v Barry Miller>再说一遍，如果这是为了弄点钱而进行的抢劫，我肯定会选一个不在繁华街道，不在警察局隔壁两个街区的地方。但无论如何，我最深刻的记忆是在抢劫银行之后，我就走在一条相对繁忙的街道上。我停车的银行停车场在后边。我的意思是，我甚至都没停在正门。我得走过四分之一个街区才能到车那儿。我手里拿着枪，脸上戴着面具，不管那是早上的几点。又不是万圣节。

00:26:48.970 --> 00:27:21.450
<v Barry Miller>The police came relatively quickly. The bag that had the money in it, the dye thing exploded. This big cop with a gun is yelling at me, get down on the ground, get down on the ground. I really didn't totally comprehend all of what he was saying, but I eventually did. And I got down on the ground. And he handcuffed me, put me into his car, and took me away.
<v Barry Miller>警察来得相当快。钱袋里的染色包爆炸了。那个拿着枪的高大警察朝我大喊，趴下，趴在地上。我并没有完全理解他说的所有话，但我最终明白了。我趴在了地上。他给我戴上手铐，把我塞进车里，带走了。

00:27:21.450 --> 00:27:59.090
<v Barry Miller>I'm no longer a rich man. I'm no longer a society person. I'm no longer the person that I was before. I killed that person. I can create this person that I now want to be. I can create a person who has more compassion, a person who wants a different lifestyle, who wants to live a different way. I lived on the top of the hill. I want to live in the valley with everybody else now.
<v Barry Miller>我不再是个有钱人。我不再是社会名流。我不再是以前的那个人了。我杀了那个人。我可以创造我现在想成为的那个人。我可以创造一个更有同情心的人，一个想要不同生活方式、想要以不同方式生活的人。我曾经住在山顶，现在我想和大家一起住在山谷里。

00:27:59.090 --> 00:28:33.100
<v Barry Miller>A guy in my position, coming from my socioeconomic background does not do what I did. You don't go and rob a bank. So therefore I have now been cast out from my family. And that which they needed from me, I guess I can't give to them. That which I was supposed to be, I'm now an embarrassment, which is rather remarkable because frankly I now am happier with myself as a person than I was during all of that time.
<v Barry Miller>像我这样社会经济背景的人是不会做这种事的。你不会去抢银行。所以我现在被家人抛弃了。我想他们需要我提供的那些东西，我可能给不了了。至于我本该成为的那个人，我现在成了羞耻。这非常值得关注，因为坦白说，比起那段日子，我现在作为一个人的自我感觉反而更快乐。

00:28:33.100 --> 00:29:12.500
<v Alex Blumberg>This is maybe the hardest thing of all to swallow. Talking to Miller, I got a familiar feeling. He sounded like a new convert to a religion or a junkie who's gone clean, like someone desperate to believe what he is saying is true. And who can blame him? When Barry Miller gets out of jail in four years, he's going to have to rebuild a life from scratch. No family, no job, 68 years old. Despite my doubts, I wanted to believe him too.
<v Alex Blumberg>这可能是最难接受的事情。和米勒交谈时，我有一种熟悉的感觉。他听起来像是一个刚皈依宗教的人，或者是戒了毒的瘾君子，像是一个迫切想相信自己所说的是真话的人。谁又能怪他呢？当巴里·米勒四年后出狱时，他将不得不从零开始重建生活。没有家庭，没有工作，68岁了。尽管我有疑虑，但我还是想相信他。

