00:30:58.010 --> 00:31:16.280
<v Ira Glass>In this act we have a story from David Cale from a show that premiered here at the Goodman Theatre here in Chicago. And in this show, David Cale comes out on stage in black jeans and a corduroy shirt. And without dressing as a woman or changing his voice in any way, he simply becomes this middle-aged, good-humored British woman named Lillian.
<v Ira Glass>在这一幕中，我们有一个来自大卫·凯尔的故事，选自他在芝加哥古德曼剧院首演的一场演出。在这场演出中，大卫·凯尔穿着黑牛仔裤和灯芯绒衬衫走上舞台。他没有装扮成女人，也没有改变声音，只是简单地变身为一名叫莉莲的中年、风趣的英国女性。

00:31:16.280 --> 00:31:41.800
<v Ira Glass>The story he tells is of how Lillian escapes the life she's in. She launches herself out of her old life in this kind of half trance, like the bank robber we heard from earlier but not quite so severe. Lillian take steps to destroy her own life while she's only kind of half conscious that that's exactly what is it that she's trying to do. And the incident that gets her out of her old life turns out to be so small and awkward and a bit disappointing, but it does the job.
<v Ira Glass>他讲述了莉莲如何逃离她所处的生活。她像我们刚才听到的那个银行抢劫犯一样，以一种半恍惚的状态将自己从旧生活中抛了出去，虽然没那么极端。莉莲采取行动毁掉自己的生活，但她只是半清醒地知道这正是她想要做的。而让她走出旧生活的事件，结果竟然是那么微小、尴尬，甚至有点令人失望，但它确实起到了作用。

00:31:41.800 --> 00:31:55.660
<v Ira Glass>Before we begin this story, a quick warning that there's some sexual content in the story. No nasty words and, of course, nothing as graphic as the network news this week, but some mild sexual content that might not be appropriate for some younger listeners.
<v Ira Glass>在故事开始前，预警一下，故事中有一些性内容。没有脏话，当然也没有本周电视新闻中那么露骨，但会有一些轻微的性内容，可能不适合年幼的听众。

00:31:55.660 --> 00:32:49.210
<v David Cale>The thought of someone else inside you is something I could never come to terms with. Keith had said to me before he went up north on that job, "I could get over somebody kissing you or cuddling, but if someone else went inside, I don't think I could ever touch you again." "Don't be dramatic," I said. "Nobody's going anywhere with me." Then almost a minute, the door shut. Keith was hardly in his precious Volvo when I meet little Jimmy in the store, 15 years my junior, with a look on his face the could drag a shipwreck up from the bottom of the ocean. "Wipe that dirty look off your face, Jimmy Foyle," I said, "and stop trying to put your fingers in my mouth. I'm a married woman."
<v David Cale>想到有别人进入你的身体，是我永远无法接受的事。基思在去北方出差前曾对我说：“如果有人吻你或拥抱你，我或许能接受，但如果有人进入过你，我想我再也无法碰你了。”“别演了，”我说，“谁会跟我发生什么。”不到一分钟，门就关上了。基思刚开着他那辆珍贵的沃尔沃离开，我就在店里遇到了小吉米，他比我小15岁，脸上那表情简直能把沉船从海底捞出来。“擦掉你脸上那副肮脏的表情，吉米·福伊尔，”我说，“别再想把手指伸进我嘴里了。我是个已婚女人。”

00:32:49.210 --> 00:33:38.190
<v David Cale>I couldn't believe the words were coming out of me. It was like my husband just flew out the window. Jimmy was really what I had in mind for a lover before I said yes to Keith. He was. He was the kind of person I always wanted when I was his age but who never seemed interested in me. I wasn't generally a Jimmy's type. Jimmys didn't generally take notice of women like me. We seemed too tame. Jimmy was a wild one. Rough around the edges, he was a bit of a devil. Didn't give things a lot of thought the way Keith did. And he was funny. Keith had nothing approaching a sense of humor. Actually, Keith was the only person I knew that didn't find me funny in any way.
<v David Cale>我简直不敢相信这些话竟然出自我的口中。就好像我丈夫刚才飞出了窗外。吉米其实就是我在答应基思之前心目中的理想情人。他确实是。他是我在那个年纪一直想要、但似乎从未对我感兴趣的那种人。我通常不是吉米的那种类型。吉米通常不会注意我这种女人。我们看起来太温顺了。吉米是个野孩子。性格粗犷，有点像个小恶魔。不像基思那样凡事想那么多。而且他很有趣。基思是我认识的人中唯一觉得我一点都不好笑的人。

00:33:38.190 --> 00:34:15.670
<v David Cale>"I can't believe that, Lillian," Jimmy said. "You're a riot." "Oh, how refreshing," I thought, "to be found funny again." It was really the idea of a Jimmy coming along that kept me from completely giving over to Keith. I've been holding out for the thought of a Jimmy for a long time. So when he aimed his eyes at me and came on so strong that day, something in me was saying, it would not be a good idea to turn Jimmy down. It'll be a little fling.
<v David Cale>“我真不敢相信，莉莲，”吉米说，“你太逗了。”“哦，太令人耳目一新了，”我心想，“我又被人觉得有趣了。”正是吉米出现的想法，让我没有完全向基思妥协。我一直暗暗期待着一个像吉米这样的人。所以那天他盯着我，攻势如此猛烈时，我内心有一个声音在说：拒绝吉米可不是个好主意。就当是一次小小的情感外遇吧。

00:34:15.670 --> 00:34:47.390
<v David Cale>You have to work out this Jimmy thing. Lillian, it's between you and yourself. Jimmy knew Keith was out of town. I think he'd even been watching the precious Volvo to see if it was still in the front. He invited me over to his house to see his lizards. "Lizards?" I said. "Yes, lizards," he said. "How peculiar," I said. "All right, Jimmy."
<v David Cale>你得处理好吉米这件事。莉莲，这是你和你自己的事。吉米知道基思不在镇上。我想他甚至一直盯着那辆珍贵的沃尔沃看它还在不在门口。他邀请我去他家看他的蜥蜴。“蜥蜴？”我说。“是的，蜥蜴，”他说。“真奇怪，”我说，“好吧，吉米。”

00:34:47.390 --> 00:35:05.680
<v David Cale>He drove his car like he just robbed the bank. We ran a red light. "Hold on to your seat, Lillian," I thought. His car didn't have safety belts. "I cut them off," he said. "They were uncomfortable." I thought about Keith. Keith wouldn't start the engine unless everyone was strapped in.
<v David Cale>他开车的样子就像刚刚抢了银行。我们闯了红灯。“抓紧你的座位，莉莲，”我想。他的车没装安全带。“我割掉了，”他说，“它们不舒服。”我想到基思。基思如果不让所有人都系好安全带，是不会发动引擎的。

00:35:05.680 --> 00:35:24.520
<v David Cale>Jimmy's house was lined with tiny aquariums. He got all excited as he told me what the various lizards were and where they came from. He was quite the authority. Outside of the store, he looked much younger. "God, Lillian," I thought, "what are you doing?"
<v David Cale>吉米的房子里摆满了小水族箱。他很兴奋地告诉我各种蜥蜴是什么、产自哪里。他简直是个专家。店外面的他看起来年轻多了。“天哪，莉莲，”我想，“你在干什么？”

00:35:24.520 --> 00:35:57.370
<v David Cale>When he made his move on me, it was so sudden. Talk about a pounce. Even the lizards scuttled behind their plastic rocks. I immediately felt like I'd been thrown into a wrestling ring. As we were rolling around on his leopard blanket, I must confess, my first thought was, "Am I really enjoying this?" He was so rough and young. There was no warming up with Jimmy. In fact, much to my surprise, it was dreadful.
<v David Cale>当他向我扑过来时，那真是太突然了。简直是猛扑。连蜥蜴都躲到了塑料岩石后面。我立刻觉得自己像被扔进了摔跤场。我们在他那条豹纹毯子上翻滚时，我必须承认，我第一个念头是：“我真的在享受这个吗？”他太粗鲁也太年轻了。吉米没有任何热身过程。事实上，令我非常惊讶的是，这太糟糕了。

00:35:57.370 --> 00:36:14.590
<v David Cale>It's funny, I realized I'd gotten used to Keith's mouth. Jimmy had a smaller mouth. I think Keith's tongue was wider, too. There was nothing sensual about Jimmy's tongue. It just sort of flickered around in my mouth, like the tongues of one of those lizards of his.
<v David Cale>真有趣，我意识到我已经习惯了基思的嘴。吉米的嘴更小。我觉得基思的舌头也更宽。吉米的舌头一点感官刺激都没有。它只是在我嘴里闪来闪去，就像他那些蜥蜴的舌头一样。

00:36:14.590 --> 00:36:41.150
<v David Cale>Whatever was wrong with Keith, the sexual part was all right. Or maybe I'd overrated it, but he was considerate. Sexually speaking, I'd say Keith was like a really good waiter in a pretty good restaurant. Very good service but ultimately disappointing food.
<v David Cale>基思不管有什么问题，性这方面还是可以的。也许是我高估了它，但他很体贴。从性方面来说，基思就像一家还不错的餐馆里非常好的服务员。服务很好，但食物最终还是令人失望。

00:36:41.150 --> 00:37:37.030
<v David Cale>Jimmy seemed to approach the whole thing like it was some form of kung fu or that I was something that needed to be overthrown. But believe me, I was just laying there. He was nervous, bless him. I tried to get him to ease up. "Slow down," I said, in a voice that was supposed to sound seductive. But I have to admit, it did come out rather motherly. Then he breathed into my ear. "Can I [BLEEP] you, Lillian?" I thought about Keith, what the thought of someone else being inside me would do to him, how it really would be the final straw. And I said, "Yes, Jimmy. If you want."
<v David Cale>吉米对待整件事的方式好像它是某种功夫，或者我是什么需要被征服的东西。但相信我，我只是躺在那儿。他很紧张，老天保佑他。我试着让他放松点。“慢点，”我用一种本该诱惑的语调说。但我不得不承认，听起来确实更像是母亲的叮嘱。然后他对着我的耳朵呼吸。“莉莲，我可以搞你吗？”我想到基思，想到如果有别人进入我的身体他会有什么反应，那真的会成为压死骆驼的最后一根稻草。我说：“可以，吉米。如果你想要的话。”

00:37:37.030 --> 00:37:49.590
<v Ira Glass>What follows is a brief scene that's too graphic to play for you on the radio. Let's just say things take awhile. Jimmy keeps his eyes scrunched shut. Lillian keeps hers open, lays there thinking about her husband, staring at the aquarium.
<v Ira Glass>接下来是一个简短的场景，过于露骨，不便在电台播出。我们只能说事情花了一些时间。吉米一直紧闭双眼。莉莲一直睁着眼，躺在那儿想着丈夫，盯着水族箱。

00:37:49.590 --> 00:38:10.920
<v David Cale>And I'm looking at Jimmy's lizards. Can they really be happy in those little tanks with a light bulb over their heads morning and night? And he took so long. [YAWNS] It is starting to feel a little like a visit to the dentist more than a sexual fling.
<v David Cale>我看着吉米的蜥蜴。它们在那充满电灯泡、日夜明亮的小水箱里真的快乐吗？他花了太长时间。（打哈欠）这开始感觉更像是看牙医，而不是什么性外遇。

00:38:10.920 --> 00:38:34.730
<v David Cale>Then Jimmy makes a noise like he's been shot in the leg. And I realize, "Thank God." He rolls over to the other side of the blanket. "That was sexy," he says. "Yes," I said. "It was very. Thank you." Lillian, polite to the end.
<v David Cale>然后吉米发出了一声像腿部中弹的声音。我意识到，“谢天谢地。”他翻身滚到毯子的另一边。“那很性感，”他说。“是的，”我说，“是非常。谢谢。”莉莲，自始至终彬彬有礼。

00:38:34.730 --> 00:39:02.760
<v David Cale>But then Jimmy did some really sweet things. He ran a bath, put something blue in the water. He showed me his muscles. "Ooh," I said. He lit candles. He was quite romantic after all. He was a boy, really. I half expected the police to come barging in and arrest me.
<v David Cale>但后来吉米做了些非常贴心的事。他放了热水，在水里加了些蓝色的东西。他向我展示了他的肌肉。“哦，”我说。他点燃了蜡烛。他毕竟还是挺浪漫的。他确实还是个孩子。我甚至半是期待警察闯进来逮捕我。

00:39:02.760 --> 00:39:14.330
<v David Cale>"Did I disappoint you?" he asked. "No, of course not," I said. And for a moment, he looked so vulnerable, I thought my heart would break.
<v David Cale>“我让你失望了吗？”他问。“没有，当然没有，”我说。有一瞬间，他看起来如此脆弱，我觉得我的心都要碎了。

00:39:14.330 --> 00:39:37.760
<v David Cale>We sat in the bath. He was behind me. He did my back. Drew objects on my shoulders in soap and had me guess what they were. "It's a giraffe," I said. "No, it's a crane," he said. "You win," I said. He was laughing. He looked even younger with his hair wet.
<v David Cale>我们坐在浴缸里。他在我身后。他帮我擦背。用肥皂在我的肩膀上画东西让我猜是什么。“是长颈鹿，”我说。“不对，是起重机，”他说。“你赢了，”我说。他在笑。头发湿漉漉的他看起来更年轻了。

00:39:37.760 --> 00:40:01.600
<v David Cale>And I'm thinking of Keith in these Volvo, following the taillights of another car with his radio on-- he probably near Scotland by then-- with that perpetually anxious look on his face that he inherited from his father. And they're both nice men. I mean, sweet men, good people.
<v David Cale>我正想着基思开着沃尔沃，跟着另一辆车的尾灯，收音机开着——他当时可能已经快到苏格兰了——脸上带着那种从他父亲那里遗传来的、永恒的焦虑表情。他们都是好男人。我是说，贴心的男人，好人。

00:40:01.600 --> 00:40:23.580
<v David Cale>And as the damn television is chattering away in the background, all I can think is, "Well, my dear, now what?"
<v David Cale>背景里电视机嘈杂声响个不停，我唯一能想到的就是：“嗯，亲爱的，现在该怎么办？”

00:40:23.580 --> 00:40:30.230
<v Ira Glass>Now we skip ahead five years. Lillian is out of her marriage, working in a bookstore. She decides to go away to Brighton for a little vacation.
<v Ira Glass>现在我们跳过五年。莉莲已经离婚，在一家书店工作。她决定去布莱顿度个假。

00:40:30.230 --> 00:41:07.540
<v David Cale>Well, I was walking along the front, past the chalets on the beach when I hear somebody calling my name. I turned around and see this young man with a beard waving at me. "Who on Earth are you," I thought. I presumed it must be a customer from the store. "It's me," said the man with the beard. "It's Jimmy. Don't you remember?" "Jimmy," I cried, totally taken aback, "You look completely different. I would have walked right past you." Besides the beard, he was much slimmer. And it's probably my imagination, but I think he'd grown.
<v David Cale>那天我正沿着海滩前面的小木屋走，突然听到有人喊我的名字。我转过身，看到一个留着胡子的年轻人向我招手。“你是谁啊，”我心想。我猜这大概是店里的顾客。“是我，”那个留胡子的男人说，“是吉米。你不记得了？”“吉米，”我喊道，完全惊呆了，“你看起来完全变了。我刚才差点直接走过你了。”除了胡子，他瘦了很多。可能是我的错觉，但我认为他长高了。

00:41:07.540 --> 00:41:43.610
<v David Cale>"What are you doing here," I asked. "I live here," he said. "Since when?" "Since about four years ago." I could not get over how different he looked. "I don't know lift weights anymore," he said. "I do tai chi. Lost a lot of bulk. Don't have muscles anymore. And I've gone vegetarian." "And you have the beard now," I chimed. "Yeah, my wife says it gives me authority." "Your wife?" "Can you believe it?" he said. "I got hitched."
<v David Cale>“你在这干什么？”我问。“我住这儿，”他说。“什么时候开始的？”“大约四年前。”我无法相信他看起来有多大的不同。“我不再举重了，”他说，“我练太极。减轻了很多体重。没肌肉了。而且我吃素了。”“而且你现在还留了胡子，”我附和道。“是啊，我老婆说这让我更有威严。”“你老婆？”“你敢相信吗？”他说，“我结婚了。”

00:41:43.610 --> 00:42:02.340
<v David Cale>The whole episode was quite disorienting. Having Jimmy suddenly pop up in the middle of my Brighton after how many years? Five, was it? He asked me if I'd like to grab a bite. I said I would. I have to say, though, it was immediately comfortable between us. One thing about Jimmy and I, we always got along.
<v David Cale>整件事让人非常迷惑。吉米在这么多年后——是五年吗？——突然出现在我的布莱顿中间。他问我想不想吃点东西。我说好。但我不得不说，我们之间立刻就感觉很自然。我和吉米有一点很好，我们一直合得来。

00:42:02.340 --> 00:42:50.300
<v David Cale>"I don't drink coffee anymore," he said. "Only herb tea. I'm watching my health, but I can have a chamomile tea and a tofu sandwich. I'm very strict." "Tofu sandwich," I said. "Oh, dear. I should be having a glass of wine, a pastry, a cappuccino, and a cigarette, in that order. Health kick." He laughed. "Oh, good," I thought. "I'm still amusing to him." While he wasn't looking, I glanced at the side of his face. He'd become quite beautiful, delicate almost. In the looks department, I have to say, the little bugger had really come into his own.
<v David Cale>“我不再喝咖啡了，”他说，“只喝药草茶。我在注意健康，但我可以喝点洋甘菊茶，吃个豆腐三明治。我非常严格。”“豆腐三明治，”我说，“哦，天哪。我应该来杯红酒、点心、卡布奇诺，最后再来支烟，按这个顺序。什么健康饮食。”他笑了。“哦，真好，”我心想，“他依然觉得我很有趣。”在他不注意的时候，我瞥了一眼他的侧脸。他变得非常漂亮，几乎精致。在相貌方面，我不得不说，这个小家伙真的脱胎换骨了。

00:42:50.300 --> 00:43:42.810
<v David Cale>We went to an awful health restaurant where they had a lot of attitude and no pastry. And when I tried to light a cigarette, I got a lecture on secondhand smoke. He wanted me to meet his wife, thought the two of us would get along. "Why," I couldn't help wondering. "She reminds me of you," he said. "She's a bit older than me, very successful in business, has her own company. She trains executives how to speak in public and how to alter their image so they'll become a more effective tool in the marketplace." The whole enterprise sounded positively creepy. I asked him what his role in all this was. He said he videotaped the executives speaking and that Donna, the wife, would identify their weaknesses and therefore help to rectify them.
<v David Cale>我们去了一家糟糕的健康餐馆，那里的服务员态度很傲慢，而且没点心。当我试图点烟时，还被上了一堂关于二手烟的课。他想让我见见他妻子，觉得我们两个会合得来。“为什么？”我不禁好奇。“她让我想起了你，”他说，“她比我大一点，事业非常成功，有自己的公司。她训练高管如何公开演讲，如何改变形象，让他们在市场上成为更有效的工具。”整个生意听起来真的很令人毛骨悚然。我问他在其中扮演什么角色。他说他负责把高管演讲的录像拍下来，然后唐娜——也就是他妻子——会指出他们的弱点，从而帮助他们改正。

00:43:42.810 --> 00:44:02.280
<v David Cale>"How old are you now," I asked, somewhat shifting the subject. "26," he said. "How old are you?" "I'm not telling you how old am," I said. "Let's put it this way. When I write down my date of birth now, I put the word circa next to it."
<v David Cale>“你现在多大了？”我问，顺便转移了话题。“26岁，”他说，“你多大了？”“我不会告诉我的年龄的，”我说，“我们这么说吧，我现在写出生日期时，旁边会加上‘大约’这个词。”

00:44:02.280 --> 00:44:36.110
<v David Cale>Then he asked if I thought he seemed more mature. Apparently Donna was working on having him project a more mature version of himself. "Have you noticed I'm speaking slower," he asked. "Donna got me to do that. She made me watch a video of myself. I had no idea I talked so quickly." I'd never noticed it. Poor Jimmy was beginning to sound a bit like someone who's had a complete nervous breakdown and who's slowly pasting themselves back together again. And I was gaining the impression he'd married Eva Braun.
<v David Cale>然后他问我，觉得他看起来是否更成熟了。显然唐娜正致力于让他展现出一个更成熟的自己。“你注意到我说话变慢了吗？”他问，“唐娜让我做到的。她让我看自己的录像。我根本不知道自己说话那么快。”我从来没注意过。可怜的吉米听起来有点像那种彻底精神崩溃、正在努力把自己一点点拼凑回去的人。而我开始觉得他娶了爱娃·布劳恩。

00:44:36.110 --> 00:45:24.850
<v David Cale>"She's not pointing any cameras at me," I thought, just as some horrendous-looking alfalfa something or other arrived. God knows what it was, but I wasn't going to eat it. He asked me how what's-his-name was. "You mean Keith," I said. "I have no idea. I haven't seen Keith for nearly two years. The last time I tried to talk to Keith, he said he still wasn't ready to speak to me yet." "Was it because of me," he asked, with a tone of slight self-satisfaction. I have to say, it did irritate me for a moment. I mean, there was a reason Keith and I stayed together for six years. It wasn't a complete waste of time. "Let's just say you didn't help."
<v David Cale>“她没拿摄像机对着我，”我心想。就在这时，一些看起来惨不忍睹的紫花苜蓿之类的东西上桌了。天知道那是什么，但我可不打算吃。他问我某某人怎么样了。“你是说基思？”我说，“不知道。我有两年没见基思了。上次我试图跟基思说话，他说他还没准备好跟我交谈。”“是因为我吗？”他带着一丝自以为是的语气问。我必须承认，那一刻确实让我恼火。我的意思是，基思和我在一起六年是有原因的。那绝不是完全浪费时间。“只能说你没帮上什么忙。”

00:45:24.850 --> 00:45:52.710
<v David Cale>One thing about Keith, he could read me like a book. He immediately knew what had happened with Jimmy. "The way you talked about him in the store, it was obvious," he'd say. "It's sad," he'd go on. "You're like a little girl. You'll fall for anyone who flatters you." Keith always referred to the thing with Jimmy as the episode. "You haven't been the same person since the episode," was how he put it.
<v David Cale>关于基思有一点，他看我就像看书一样。他立刻就知道了吉米发生的事。“你在店里谈论他的方式，太明显了，”他会说，“真可悲，”他继续说，“你就像个小女孩。你会爱上任何恭维你的人。”基思总是把吉米的事称为“那次插曲”。“自从那次插曲后，你就不是以前的你了，”他是这么说的。

00:45:52.710 --> 00:46:27.130
<v David Cale>I really didn't want to meet Donna. I imagined her as exuding sex appeal and confidence, and I really wasn't feeling up to comparisons. But I went along with it. For some reason, Jimmy was so eager for us to meet. He called her up, warned her we were on our way while I excused myself for an emergency one-on-one between myself and my face in the bathroom mirror. "I hope it's the light in here," I thought. "Either that, my dear, or it is time to lay off the lattes."
<v David Cale>我真的不想见唐娜。我想象她一定散发着性感和自信，而我真的不想进行比较。但我还是去了。不知何故，吉米非常渴望我们见面。他给她打了电话，警告她我们正在去的路上，而我借口去洗手间镜子前做了一次紧急自我面容修复。“希望是因为光线，”我心想，“不然就是，亲爱的，你真的该少喝拿铁了。”

00:46:27.130 --> 00:47:19.660
<v David Cale>Donna was on a business call when we walked in, with her back to us. Well, she was nothing at all like I'd anticipated. Donna was a big girl. I mean literally. She must have been at least six feet two. And I have to say-- and this will sound awfully ungenerous on my part, and it is-- but the first thing that drew my attention was her hips. She's probably given birth to something, I presumed. When she turned around, I thought, "Oh, I'm much better looking than you." And I was sort of surprised at myself for how juvenile I was behaving. But I have to say, I did suddenly feel in the mood to be a little threatening.
<v David Cale>我们走进去时，唐娜正在打业务电话，背对着我们。好吧，她和我预想的完全不同。唐娜是个大个子。我是说字面意思上的。她至少有一米八八。我不得不说——这听起来会让我显得很刻薄，事实也确实如此——但我注意到的第一件事是她的臀部。我想，她可能生过什么东西吧。当她转过身时，我想：“哦，我比你好看多了。”我自己都惊讶于自己竟然表现得这么幼稚。但我必须说，我突然有种想挑衅一下的冲动。

00:47:19.660 --> 00:47:57.070
<v David Cale>Finally she gets off the phone, waltzes over, extending her hand. "I'm sorry," she says. "The Americans just think I've got all the time in the world. But they're where the money is, so can't be complaining too much now, can we?" Then she kissed Jimmy on the mouth in front of me, which seemed a bit unnecessary, and said, "Hello, James." "James," I thought. "James. Well, excuse me." Jimmy may have been many things. One thing he wasn't was a James.
<v David Cale>终于她挂了电话，优雅地走过来，伸出手。“对不起，”她说，“美国人总觉得我有大把的时间。但钱都在他们那儿，所以也不能太抱怨了，不是吗？”然后她在我的注视下吻了吉米的嘴，这看起来有点没必要，她说：“你好，詹姆斯。”“詹姆斯，”我想，“詹姆斯。好吧，打扰了。”吉米可能是个多种多样的人，但有一点他绝不是，他不是詹姆斯。

00:47:57.070 --> 00:48:25.600
<v David Cale>Redirecting her attention towards me, "What line are you in, Lillian," she asks. "Books," I say, "though I seldom read them." "Oh. Publishing," she says. "No," says I. "Secondhand mostly." "Oh," she says. "What a pretty shirt you're wearing." And I thought, "You cow."
<v David Cale>她把注意力转回我身上，“你是干哪行的，莉莲？”她问。“书，”我说，“尽管我很少读它们。”“哦。出版业？”她说。“不，”我说，“主要卖二手书。”“哦，”她说，“你穿的衬衫真漂亮。”我心想：“你这贱货。”

00:48:25.600 --> 00:49:07.950
<v David Cale>I was only there for about 10 minutes, which was quite long enough. Donna said she had a meeting to go to. "Oh, AA?" I inquired. "Oh, you're hilarious," she said. "So are you," I replied, which got no response. She said she was going to Germany for the rest of the week and that James had the car. Every time she mentioned James, I had to think for a moment who she was talking about. "You two can get reacquainted," she said. "You don't need me around, now, do you?" For a second, it felt like she was throwing him at me.
<v David Cale>我在那儿只呆了10分钟左右，已经足够久了。唐娜说她要去开个会。“哦，戒酒互助会？”我问。“你真幽默，”她说。“你也一样，”我回答，她没有回应。她说她这周剩下的时间要去德国，詹姆斯有车。每次她提到詹姆斯，我都要想一会儿她在说谁。“你们俩可以重温旧情，”她说，“不需要我在旁边，不是吗？”有一瞬间，感觉她像是要把他扔给我。

00:49:07.950 --> 00:49:42.960
<v David Cale>Jimmy wanted to give me a tour of the south coast. So the following day, after Donna left, he picked me up at the hotel. We went driving in her BMW. "What happened to your car?" I asked. "Donna made me get rid of it," he said. "I liked that car," I protested. "It suited you." "She approves of you," he said. "She thought you were down to earth," which, from a pretentious person, is not a compliment.
<v David Cale>吉米想带我游览南海岸。所以第二天，唐娜走后，他开车来酒店接我。我们开着她的宝马。你的车呢？我问。“唐娜让我把它处理了，”他说。“我喜欢那辆车，”我抗议道，“它很适合你。”“她认可你，”他说，“她觉得你脚踏实地。”——这话从一个自命不凡的人嘴里说出来，可不是什么恭维。

00:49:42.960 --> 00:50:04.970
<v David Cale>"She said she thinks you're the kind of woman she could imagine me being with much more than her." "What does she mean by that?" I asked. "I don't know," he said. "Sometimes I think she's tired of being married to people." "I'm her fourth husband." "Oh," I said. Then we just go drove for a while without saying anything.
<v David Cale>“她说她觉得你是我能想象到的，比她更适合在一起的那种女人。”“她是什么意思？”我问。“我不知道，”他说，“有时我觉得她厌倦了和人结婚。我是她的第四任丈夫。”“哦，”我说。然后我们就开车走了一会儿，谁也没说话。

00:50:04.970 --> 00:51:33.970
<v David Cale>The countryside along the south coast is lovely. I remembered how much I like to be in the passenger seat of a car. While Jimmy was driving, I happened to notice his hands on the steering wheel. They looked older. I looked at mine. I'm sure they do too. He slipped in a cassette tape. I lowered my window. A gust of wind blew through the car. My hair went everywhere. Care and all its relatives seemed to fall out of me. I don't know if it was the sea air or the music, too many cappuccinos or Jimmy, but I felt something like a sudden wave of enthusiasm. He must have thought my mind had just fluttered out the car window. "Right now," I explained, "I feel like I'm right in the world."
<v David Cale>南海岸的风景很美。我记得我有多喜欢坐在汽车副驾驶的位置。吉米开车时，我无意中看到他放在方向盘上的手。它们看起来老了。我看了看自己的。我相信它们也老了。他塞进一盘磁带。我摇下车窗。一阵风吹进车里。我的头发被吹得乱七八糟。忧虑及其亲戚似乎从我身上消失了。我不知道是因为海风、音乐、喝了太多卡布奇诺还是吉米，但我感到一阵热情涌上心头。他一定以为我的心随风飘走了。“此刻，”我解释说，“我觉得自己就在这个世界上。”

00:51:33.970 --> 00:52:06.570
<v David Cale>Driving along the coast, we came to a huge fun fair. Jimmy wanted to get off the road and investigate. I did not take much convincing. As we were going in, he stopped in his tracks. "I always get excited around you," he said. I said, "I feel the same way." I realized what it was. You see, you can be romantic with Jimmy and not feel like a fool. I had been waiting a long time to be romantic with someone.
<v David Cale>沿着海岸开，我们看到了一个巨大的游乐场。吉米想下车去探索一下。我没怎么推辞就同意了。进门的时候，他停下脚步。“在你身边我总是很兴奋，”他说。我说，“我感觉也一样。”我意识到那是什么了。你看，你可以和吉米浪漫而不觉得自己是个傻瓜。我等这一天等了很久。

00:52:06.570 --> 00:52:43.780
<v David Cale>Jimmy was immediately drawn to an enormous roller coaster called the Big Plunge. The Big Plunge was a ride that would go up extremely high and then plummet. Now, I have a terrible fear of heights. "There is no way you're going to get me up on that thing," I said. "You go if you want to. I'll stay here and have a cigarette." "Oh, Lillian, you're afraid of everything," he said. "How dare you?" I said. "I'm not." "Come on," he said. "Fear at a certain point just becomes another bad habit." Which had to have been something he'd picked up from Donna.
<v David Cale>吉米立刻被一个巨大的过山车吸引住了，叫“大俯冲”。“大俯冲”是一个会升到极高处然后猛地坠落的游乐设施。现在，我特别恐高。“你不可能让我坐那个东西，”我说，“你想去就去吧。我就在这儿抽根烟。”“哦，莉莲，你什么都怕，”他说。“你竟敢？”我说，“我才没有。”“来吧，”他说，“恐惧到了某种程度就只是一种坏习惯。”这肯定是他在唐娜那里学到的。

00:52:43.780 --> 00:53:10.010
<v David Cale>The only way I could go on the Big Plunge was by going through a complicated psychological snow job in which I rationalize that my fear of heights actually represented my terror of life and that going on the Big Plunge was extremely important for me and could lead to a personal breakthrough. Besides if 10-year-old children could brave it, so could I. "I'm sure I'm the oldest person to ever take the plunge," I cried. The terrified wit.
<v David Cale>我能坐上“大俯冲”的唯一方法，就是通过复杂的心理暗示，合理化说我对高度的恐惧实际上代表了我对生活的恐惧，坐这个对我来说非常重要，可能导致个人的突破。再说，如果10岁小孩都敢坐，我也可以。“我相信我是有史以来坐这个年纪最大的人了，”我大声说，这是我害怕时的自嘲。

00:53:10.010 --> 00:53:40.900
<v David Cale>As the ride yanked everyone forward and the squealing began, first off I was petrified. Then I seemed to push through the fear. I have to say, the whole thing did seem like a personal breakthrough. Donna would have been proud of me. Jimmy held my hand for the duration, which was sweet of him. I've never been able to go on those rides before, but I ended up loving it. Went on three more times. It had become completely easy between us.
<v David Cale>当游乐设施向前猛冲，尖叫声响起时，我起初吓坏了。然后我似乎冲破了恐惧。我不得不说，整件事看起来确实像是一种个人突破。唐娜要是看到肯定会为我骄傲。吉米全程握着我的手，这真是太贴心了。我以前从没坐过这种设施，但最后我爱上了它。又坐了三次。我们之间变得完全轻松自如了。

00:53:40.900 --> 00:54:10.780
<v David Cale>Midway through the day, a strange notion crossed my mind. "I can imagine myself being with you for a long time," I thought. By late afternoon, I realized I really was getting a little lightheaded around Jimmy. I stood at a distance as he was buying tickets for the next ride and tried to give myself a little talking to. "He's 26. He's married. He lives in Brighton," I said to myself. "Lillian, stop it."
<v David Cale>在这一天快结束时，一个奇怪的念头闪过脑海。“我可以想象自己和你在一起很长一段时间，”我想。到了傍晚，我意识到自己在吉米身边确实有点头晕目眩。在他买下一趟票时，我站在远处，试着给自己做了一番心理建设。“他26岁，他结婚了，他住在布莱顿，”我对自己说，“莉莲，别这样。”

00:54:10.780 --> 00:54:29.210
<v David Cale>Then out of the blue, on the Ferris wheel, he gives me a peck on the cheek. "What are you doing?" I asked, trying to not sound delighted and failing abysmally. "I wanted to kiss you," he says. "James," I scolded, "behave."
<v David Cale>然后出人意料地，在摩天轮上，他在我脸颊上亲了一下。“你在干什么？”我问，努力不让自己表现出狂喜，但惨败了。“我想吻你，”他说。“詹姆斯，”我训斥道，“规矩点。”

00:54:29.210 --> 00:55:07.040
<v David Cale>The Ferris wheel then abruptly jolted to a halt, fortunately with us on the low end. While we were waiting to move, Jimmy came out with what seemed like an out of the blue question. "If somebody told you your life would end in, say, a year, do you think you'd start to really live or do you think you'd slip back under the covers and wait for it to be over?" "I think I'd get a move on," I replied. Just then the wheel began to turn. As we were passing the arcade, I noticed a photo booth. "I don't have any pictures of you," I said. "Come on. I need some proof that you exist."
<v David Cale>摩天轮突然猛地停住了，幸好我们在底部。在我们等待移动时，吉米问了一个突如其来的问题。“如果有人告诉你，你的生命只剩下一年，你觉得你会开始真正生活，还是会躲回被子里等待结束？”“我想我会立刻行动起来，”我回答。就在那时轮子开始转动。当我们经过拱廊时，我注意到一个照相亭。“我一张你的照片都没有，”我说，“来吧，我需要证明你真实存在。”

00:55:07.040 --> 00:55:35.280
<v David Cale>We were clowning around in front of the camera. Pictures took forever to come out. When they finally did emerge from the machine, I wasn't quite prepared for what I saw. What took me back about the photos was that we looked like two people who were completely in love with each other.
<v David Cale>我们在镜头前耍宝。照片洗出来花了好久。当它们最终从机器里出来时，我对自己所看到的感到有点措手不及。让我惊讶的是，照片里看起来就像是一对正完全坠入爱河的人。

00:55:35.280 --> 00:55:39.450
<v Ira Glass>It's a year later. Lillian and Jimmy are married and happy.
<v Ira Glass>一年后。莉莲和吉米结婚了，生活得很幸福。

00:55:39.450 --> 00:56:26.160
<v David Cale>He had next to nothing in the way of possessions. Everything had belonged to Donna. One of the few things that was actually his was this little tent that he took everywhere just in case he ever felt the urge to sleep outside, he'd say. One Saturday, we took a bus ride into the country. Sure enough, little package came along too. No sooner we're off the bus than Jimmy said, "Let's forget about going home. I want to sleep under the sky." I have to say, the idea did appeal. So we bought some sandwiches and supplies, walked what must have been miles till we found this spot that met Jimmy's definition of the word remote, by which time the sky was quite black. He set up a little tent and pulled out a sleeping bag he'd had all along.
<v David Cale>他几乎一无所有。一切都是唐娜的。他为数不多的私人物品之一是这个小帐篷，他去哪都带着，说万一哪天想露宿街头就用得着。一个星期六，我们坐公交车进城。果不其然，小包裹也跟着去了。刚下车，吉米就说：“别回去了，我想睡在天空下。”不得不说，这个想法很吸引人。于是我们买了些三明治和补给品，走了好几英里，直到找到一个符合吉米所谓“偏远”定义的地点，当时天空已经全黑了。他搭了个小帐篷，拿出他一直带在身边的睡袋。

00:56:26.160 --> 00:57:01.810
<v David Cale>By the time we'd eaten, it was late. Jimmy went pretty much straight to sleep. I, on the other hand, just laid there. The ground was uneven to put it mildly. I think we were parked on top of a mole community. My spine was wondering what the hell was going on, and it was damp. But I didn't care. It was so quiet in the open. You could almost hear your moods change. If you laid still enough, you could feel your instincts wriggling to the surface.
<v David Cale>等我们吃完，天已经晚了。吉米很快就睡着了。我却一直躺着。地面很不平坦，说得委婉点。我想我们是停在一个鼹鼠社区的顶部。我的脊椎在想这到底是怎么回事，而且地面很潮湿。但我不在乎。户外太安静了。你几乎能听到你情绪的变化。如果你躺得够平，你甚至能感觉到你的本能正在蠕动浮现。

00:57:01.810 --> 00:57:48.670
<v David Cale>Hours go by, and I'm still wide awake, thinking, "I'm 41 years old. It's 3 o'clock in the morning. And I'm laying in a field." And the whole thing seemed so absurd. I just started laughing. "You know what?" I thought. "Good things are going to happen. I'm ready for them now. I wasn't before. I am now." "Yes," I thought. "I'm ready to roll my sleeves up. My sleeves are fairly twitching to be rolled for the good things."
<v David Cale>几个小时过去了，我依然清醒着，心想：“我41岁了。现在是凌晨3点。我躺在田野里。”整件事看起来如此荒谬。我不禁笑了起来。“你知道吗？”我想，“好事要发生了。我现在准备好了。以前没有，现在准备好了。”“是的，”我想，“我准备好卷起袖子了。我的袖子都在为好事而跃跃欲试。”

00:57:48.670 --> 00:57:59.250
<v Ira Glass>David Cale's one woman show Lillian premiered at the Goodman Studio Theatre in Chicago. It was directed by Joe Mantello. They're currently in the process of putting the show up in other cities. Watch for it. It's really great.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·凯尔的单人秀《莉莲》在芝加哥古德曼工作室剧院首演。由乔·曼特洛执导。他们目前正在筹划在其他城市巡演。留意一下。真的很棒。

